---
/**
 * The method, in first person.
 *
 * Rewritten August 2026 against a voice audit. The old version was accurate
 * and sounded like nobody wrote it: royal "we" throughout, an em dash every
 * other sentence, and a neat epigram closing each section. For a page whose
 * whole argument is "a person went and checked", having no person in it was a
 * structural contradiction.
 *
 * Constraints this page is written to, and the next ones should be too:
 *   - first person singular for anything I actually did
 *   - em dashes at roughly one per 300 words, not one per two sentences
 *   - no three-item lists in consecutive paragraphs
 *   - no aphorism resolving a section; end on the fact and stop
 *   - paragraph lengths vary hard, one-sentence paragraphs allowed
 *   - the mistakes are named, with dates, because they are the evidence
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Who writes this site?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Matt Werth. I make the calls, read the source documents, and drive out to the parks. Corrections are published under my name with the date.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Where do the facts come from?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "From the body that sets them: Maryland DNR, the county, the town. Never from another guide or a review site. A community write-up can point me at something but cannot carry a page on its own.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What happens when something is wrong?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "I fix it, date it, and leave the mistake on the page saying what it used to say. Corrections are published, not deleted.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How I check this — Deep Creek Field Guide"
  description="Who writes this site, where the facts come from, and the three things I have already got wrong and corrected in public. Every fact carries a date."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">Method</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[13ch] text-slate">How I check this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[56ch]">
      I go to whoever sets the rule, write down the date I asked, and publish it
      when I get it wrong.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      My name is Matt Werth. I don't live in Garrett County. I've been going out
      there for forty years, first as a camper at the 4-H centre and later as a
      volunteer, and I still drive out with my kids. That's the honest version
      of my standing here: not a local, but not a stranger either.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I'm an engineer by trade. It shows. This site is mostly tables and dates,
      because I don't trust my own memory of a park I visited two summers ago
      and I don't expect you to trust it either.
    </p>

    <h2>Why any of this is necessary</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Swallow Falls started requiring a day-use reservation in May. Months on,
      plenty of what you'll find still tells you to turn up and park. Nobody is
      being careless. It's that no one re-checks a page they wrote three years
      ago, and almost nobody wrote down where the fact came from in the first
      place.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      From Baltimore that mistake costs you three hours.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>

    <h3>1. The source is whoever sets the rule</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      A park rule comes from the park or the agency running it. A town park's
      hours come from the town. A fee comes from whoever takes the money. I
      don't take facts from other guides, including when they're probably
      right.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Every page says how solid the fact is</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There are two honest answers to "how do you know that", so they're
      labelled separately instead of blurred into one word.
    </p>

    <dl class="sheet breakout my-6 divide-y-2 divide-lake/25">
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-flag-green-bearing">Confirmed</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          I called, visited, or asked the people who set the rule. The stamp
          names them and gives the date.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-contour-deep">Read from the source</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          Taken from a named primary source, linked so you can check it
          yourself. Maryland DNR's park page, the county's own notice, a town's
          own listing. Accurate as far as it goes, and not the same as having
          asked.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Both publish. The difference is stated because "DNR publishes this" and "I
      rang DNR and asked" are different claims, and you're entitled to know
      which one you're reading. Most of this site is currently the second kind.
    </p>

    <h3>3. What never goes in</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      A fact whose only origin is another guide, a review site or a tourism
      write-up. It gets marked <code class="stamp">noindex</code> and stays out
      of the sitemap until someone has actually checked it.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That's enforced by the build rather than by me remembering. I'll come back
      to why that distinction matters, because for a while it wasn't true.
    </p>

    <h3>4. "Not confirmed" is a real answer</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Where I don't know something the page says so. An empty field means I
      haven't checked it. It does not mean the park lacks the thing, and
      rendering it as "No" would invent facts ten at a time.
    </p>

    <h3>5. When sources disagree, whoever sets the rule wins</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I don't average two sources or split the difference. There's a live
      example. Garrett County's May announcement says Swallow Falls needs a
      reservation every day; Maryland DNR says weekends and holidays, in three
      separate places. DNR runs the park and takes the bookings, so DNR wins and
      the county's version sits on the page as a recorded conflict.
    </p>

    <h3 id="corrections">6. I publish the mistakes</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      When I get something wrong I fix it, date it, and leave the old claim on
      the page saying what it used to say. A site that promises verification and
      quietly rewrites its errors is worth less than one that never promised.
    </p>

    <h2>Three I've already got wrong</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      This is the part I'd want to read first if I landed here, so it isn't
      buried.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <strong>Swallow Falls reservations.</strong> I published "every day,
      weekends included" on the strength of the county's announcement. DNR runs
      the park and says weekends and holidays. A reader caught it.
      <a href="/parks/swallow-falls/#corrected">The correction is on the page</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <strong>The 4-H centre.</strong> I had it as a lake and beach open daily
      through the summer for swimming and canoeing. University of Maryland
      Extension, who run it, put swimming and boating on the private side:
      programme participants and booked groups only. My version would have sent
      a family out there to swim in water they couldn't reach.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <strong>Three parks marked "believed free".</strong> Believed by whom? I
      hadn't checked. I'd written a guess in a soft enough voice that it looked
      like a fact, which is exactly the thing this site is supposed to refuse.
      They now say nobody has checked.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The 4-H error had a cause worth naming. Its only source was a county
      tourism website, and the build gate at the time checked that <em>some</em>
      source existed rather than a primary one. So the promise in rule 3 was
      decoration for about a week. The gate now requires a primary source and
      three thin records dropped out of search the moment I fixed it.
    </p>

    <h2>Found something wrong?</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Tell me. Locals know these places better than I do, and a note from
      someone who stood there yesterday beats another phone call. There's a
      report box at the bottom of every park page. Or reach me through
      <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth Design</a>, which is
      my studio and which builds and pays for this.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
      <li><a href="/parks/">The state parks</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
